Name who sent an unrecognized selector.

doesNotRecognizeSelector: already prints the class, the selector and the whole superclass chain,
which says WHERE the method should have come from. It cannot say WHO asked for it, and that is the
half that matters when the sender catches the exception: the raise then leaves no trace anywhere,
and the application simply does less than it should.

MoneyMoney loses its entire Preferences window to exactly this. showPreferences: builds the window,
sets it on the controller, and then raises on one selector; cocotron wraps action delivery in @try,
so the raise is swallowed, the window is never ordered front, and the process runs on for thousands
more log lines with ZERO occurrences of the word Exception. The three existing lines named
MMHelpButton and setHasPriority: and still could not say which code sent it.

With CIDER_TRACE_UNRECOGNIZED set, one run answers it:

    cider: UNRECOGNIZED -[MMHelpButton setHasPriority:]
    cider:   sent by:
    0   CoreFoundation  -[NSObject(NSObject) doesNotRecognizeSelector:] + 360
    1   CoreFoundation  ___forwarding___ + 623
    3   MoneyMoney      MoneyMoney + 1139901
    4   MoneyMoney      MoneyMoney + 1126337
    5   AppKit          -[NSApplication sendAction:to:from:] + 98
    6   AppKit          -[NSMenu performKeyEquivalent:] + 819

Frame 4 resolves to showPreferences: + 1825 against the objc metadata, which placed the loss inside
that method rather than anywhere else in the run, and frame 3 led to the exact call site.

OFF BY DEFAULT, on both the class and the instance variant. This fires on a path that is already
throwing and an application is entitled to probe for a selector by sending it, so a backtrace per
raise would be noise in every other run.

Task #254.

